1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,263
It is the fifth of December,

2
00:00:02,263 --> 00:00:04,149
somewhere high in the Alps.

3
00:00:04,149 --> 00:00:07,166
The sun went down at half past four,

4
00:00:07,166 --> 00:00:10,560
and every door in the village is already bolted.

5
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:12,727
You're seven years old.

6
00:00:12,727 --> 00:00:16,702
And out in the street, you can hear it.

7
00:00:16,702 --> 00:00:18,469
Bells. Heavy iron bells.

8
00:00:18,469 --> 00:00:23,328
And under the bells, the drag of a chain on frozen

9
00:00:23,328 --> 00:00:23,770
stone.

10
00:00:24,170 --> 00:00:28,192
Something with goat horns, black fur and a tongue as long

11
00:00:28,192 --> 00:00:31,483
as your arm is walking from house to house.

12
00:00:31,483 --> 00:00:34,042
It carries a bundle of birch sticks.

13
00:00:34,042 --> 00:00:36,602
And on its back there's a basket,

14
00:00:36,602 --> 00:00:38,430
big enough for a child.

15
00:00:38,830 --> 00:00:40,924
Tonight, it's looking for you.

16
00:00:40,924 --> 00:00:42,598
Not for being evil.

17
00:00:42,598 --> 00:00:43,855
For being naughty.

18
00:00:43,855 --> 00:00:48,460
And the old stories are very clear about what happens to

19
00:00:48,460 --> 00:00:51,810
naughty children who end up in that basket.

20
00:00:52,210 --> 00:00:54,307
Some say he drowns them.

21
00:00:54,307 --> 00:00:56,405
Some say he eats them.

22
00:00:56,405 --> 00:01:00,180
Some say he carries them straight down to Hell.

23
00:01:00,580 --> 00:01:02,462
But here's the thing.

24
00:01:02,462 --> 00:01:07,168
Alpine families have survived this night for hundreds of years.

25
00:01:07,168 --> 00:01:08,579
They have rules.

26
00:01:08,579 --> 00:01:09,991
Five of them.

27
00:01:09,991 --> 00:01:14,226
Follow them, and you'll see the sixth of December.

28
00:01:14,226 --> 00:01:16,108
Break one, and, well.

29
00:01:16,108 --> 00:01:17,520
Let's find out.

30
00:01:17,920 --> 00:01:20,675
This is the Ward Book.

31
00:01:20,675 --> 00:01:23,980
And tonight, how to survive Krampus.

32
00:01:24,380 --> 00:01:26,370
First, know your enemy.

33
00:01:26,370 --> 00:01:29,356
Krampus belongs to the Eastern Alps.

34
00:01:29,356 --> 00:01:34,332
Austria, Bavaria, South Tyrol, and the mountain valleys around them,

35
00:01:34,332 --> 00:01:37,815
where winter nights are long and very,

36
00:01:37,815 --> 00:01:38,810
very dark.

37
00:01:39,210 --> 00:01:43,125
His name probably comes from the old German word Krampen,

38
00:01:43,125 --> 00:01:44,300
which means claw.

39
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:48,607
Which tells you a lot about how this relationship is going

40
00:01:48,607 --> 00:01:49,390
to go.

41
00:01:49,790 --> 00:01:53,206
He's usually drawn half goat, half demon.

42
00:01:53,206 --> 00:01:55,158
Horns. Hooves. Shaggy fur.

43
00:01:55,158 --> 00:01:57,598
A long, pointed, ridiculous tongue.

44
00:01:57,598 --> 00:02:02,966
Chains around his body, and bells that you hear long before

45
00:02:02,966 --> 00:02:04,430
you see him.

46
00:02:04,830 --> 00:02:07,089
And he carries a rute.

47
00:02:07,089 --> 00:02:10,702
A bundle of birch branches, for swatting children.

48
00:02:10,702 --> 00:02:13,413
Some families say it's a warning.

49
00:02:13,413 --> 00:02:15,671
Some say it's a punishment.

50
00:02:15,671 --> 00:02:17,930
The children say it's both.

51
00:02:18,330 --> 00:02:21,235
But the rod is not the worst part.

52
00:02:21,235 --> 00:02:24,502
The worst part is the basket on his back.

53
00:02:24,502 --> 00:02:27,770
That's the exit for kids who are beyond saving.

54
00:02:28,170 --> 00:02:32,280
Now here's the strangest part of the whole legend.

55
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:34,107
Krampus never travels alone.

56
00:02:34,107 --> 00:02:36,390
He works for a saint.

57
00:02:36,790 --> 00:02:41,258
On the sixth of December, the Church celebrates Saint Nicholas,

58
00:02:41,258 --> 00:02:43,939
the kind bishop who brings presents.

59
00:02:43,939 --> 00:02:47,067
And on the eve of his feast,

60
00:02:47,067 --> 00:02:50,642
the fifth, Nicholas goes from house to house.

61
00:02:50,642 --> 00:02:53,770
Good children get apples, nuts and sweets.

62
00:02:54,170 --> 00:02:55,998
And the naughty ones?

63
00:02:55,998 --> 00:02:58,740
That's what the assistant is for.

64
00:02:58,740 --> 00:03:01,025
The saint does the rewards.

65
00:03:01,025 --> 00:03:03,310
The demon does the paperwork.

66
00:03:03,710 --> 00:03:08,020
Ward one. Know which night it is.

67
00:03:08,420 --> 00:03:12,308
Krampusnacht, Krampus Night, is the fifth of December.

68
00:03:12,308 --> 00:03:13,767
That's his night.

69
00:03:13,767 --> 00:03:16,197
Not Christmas Eve, not Halloween.

70
00:03:16,197 --> 00:03:19,599
The fifth. Everywhere else in the world,

71
00:03:19,599 --> 00:03:22,029
this is a normal Saturday.

72
00:03:22,029 --> 00:03:26,890
In the Alps, it's the night the doors get bolted.

73
00:03:27,290 --> 00:03:30,142
So the first rule is simply this.

74
00:03:30,142 --> 00:03:34,216
If you're anywhere in the Eastern Alps in early December,

75
00:03:34,216 --> 00:03:35,439
check the calendar.

76
00:03:35,439 --> 00:03:38,291
If it says five, be indoors early,

77
00:03:38,291 --> 00:03:41,958
be nice to your siblings, and for heaven's sake,

78
00:03:41,958 --> 00:03:43,180
eat your vegetables.

79
00:03:43,580 --> 00:03:48,480
Ward two. Never meet Krampus without the saint.

80
00:03:48,880 --> 00:03:52,995
In the old house visits, the two arrive together.

81
00:03:52,995 --> 00:03:56,196
Nicholas walks in first, calm and holy.

82
00:03:56,196 --> 00:03:58,939
Krampus comes in behind him, rattling,

83
00:03:58,939 --> 00:04:02,140
growling, and very much on a leash.

84
00:04:02,540 --> 00:04:04,771
And look at those chains.

85
00:04:04,771 --> 00:04:09,678
Many folklorists read them as a symbol of the Christian Church

86
00:04:09,678 --> 00:04:11,016
binding the Devil.

87
00:04:11,016 --> 00:04:12,801
Krampus is dangerous, yes.

88
00:04:12,801 --> 00:04:15,031
But he's a chained danger.

89
00:04:15,031 --> 00:04:18,600
He goes where the saint lets him go.

90
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:23,490
So as long as Saint Nicholas is in the room,

91
00:04:23,490 --> 00:04:25,734
you are, technically, under protection.

92
00:04:25,734 --> 00:04:30,224
The danger is the stories where Krampus turns up first.

93
00:04:30,224 --> 00:04:32,020
Alone. Without his boss.

94
00:04:32,420 --> 00:04:36,350
Ward three. Have your answer ready.

95
00:04:36,750 --> 00:04:39,538
When the saint arrives, he asks questions.

96
00:04:39,538 --> 00:04:41,927
Have you been good this year?

97
00:04:41,927 --> 00:04:43,918
Do you know your prayers?

98
00:04:43,918 --> 00:04:46,308
Have you listened to your parents?

99
00:04:46,308 --> 00:04:50,689
In some families, the bishop even reads from a big golden

100
00:04:50,689 --> 00:04:52,680
book of everything you did.

101
00:04:53,080 --> 00:04:55,919
Children were expected to say a prayer,

102
00:04:55,919 --> 00:04:59,569
or a poem, or at least promise to behave.

103
00:04:59,569 --> 00:05:01,597
A good answer means presents.

104
00:05:01,597 --> 00:05:04,841
A bad answer means the saint steps aside,

105
00:05:04,841 --> 00:05:07,680
and lets the assistant have a word.

106
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:09,707
So rehearse. Out loud.

107
00:05:09,707 --> 00:05:11,740
In front of a mirror.

108
00:05:11,740 --> 00:05:16,213
You are about to have the most important job interview of

109
00:05:16,213 --> 00:05:19,060
your life, and the interviewer has horns.

110
00:05:19,460 --> 00:05:23,930
Ward four. Keep the golden rod on the wall.

111
00:05:24,330 --> 00:05:27,208
Here's a detail most people never hear.

112
00:05:27,208 --> 00:05:31,731
In some Alpine homes, the birch rute isn't thrown away after

113
00:05:31,731 --> 00:05:36,253
Krampus Night. It gets painted gold and hung up in the

114
00:05:36,253 --> 00:05:38,720
house, where it stays all year.

115
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:40,921
Why? It's a reminder.

116
00:05:40,921 --> 00:05:43,623
Every time you misbehave in, say,

117
00:05:43,623 --> 00:05:46,325
July, somebody points at the wall.

118
00:05:46,325 --> 00:05:47,676
No words needed.

119
00:05:47,676 --> 00:05:52,629
The demon may be gone, but his invoice is still hanging

120
00:05:52,629 --> 00:05:53,980
in the kitchen.

121
00:05:54,380 --> 00:05:58,406
Think of it as a ward that works in reverse.

122
00:05:58,406 --> 00:06:00,419
It doesn't keep Krampus out.

123
00:06:00,419 --> 00:06:03,640
It keeps you from ever needing that basket.

124
00:06:04,040 --> 00:06:07,827
Ward five. On the night of the run,

125
00:06:07,827 --> 00:06:09,720
stay off the street.

126
00:06:10,120 --> 00:06:14,873
Today, in many Alpine towns, Krampus doesn't just visit houses.

127
00:06:14,873 --> 00:06:18,199
He runs. The Krampuslauf, the Krampus run,

128
00:06:18,199 --> 00:06:23,427
is a parade of young men in fur suits and hand-carved

129
00:06:23,427 --> 00:06:28,180
wooden masks, charging through the streets with bells and torches.

130
00:06:28,580 --> 00:06:32,405
Some of those masks take weeks to carve and weigh several

131
00:06:32,405 --> 00:06:36,229
kilos. The suits are made of real goat or sheep fur.

132
00:06:36,229 --> 00:06:40,054
And quite a few of the runners have had a few

133
00:06:40,054 --> 00:06:42,140
glasses of schnapps to keep warm.

134
00:06:42,540 --> 00:06:44,688
And the rods are real.

135
00:06:44,688 --> 00:06:48,555
Runners swat at the crowd, usually on the legs.

136
00:06:48,555 --> 00:06:53,282
Locals know to stand back, or to stand behind someone taller.

137
00:06:53,282 --> 00:06:55,430
Tourists learn the hard way.

138
00:06:55,830 --> 00:06:59,415
So the final ward is the most modern one.

139
00:06:59,415 --> 00:07:01,805
Krampus Night is a party now.

140
00:07:01,805 --> 00:07:05,390
But it's a party where the guests carry sticks.

141
00:07:05,790 --> 00:07:08,893
So where did Krampus really come from?

142
00:07:08,893 --> 00:07:11,553
This is where it gets interesting,

143
00:07:11,553 --> 00:07:13,770
because nobody is completely sure.

144
00:07:14,170 --> 00:07:19,217
In nineteen fifty-eight, the folklorist Maurice Bruce wrote that Krampus in

145
00:07:19,217 --> 00:07:23,805
Styria, a region of Austria, looked much older than Christianity.

146
00:07:23,805 --> 00:07:28,852
A horned winter spirit, from the days when mid-winter belonged to

147
00:07:28,852 --> 00:07:29,770
the dark.

148
00:07:30,170 --> 00:07:31,980
Others are more careful.

149
00:07:31,980 --> 00:07:36,957
The clearer evidence comes from Christian Nicholas plays and house visits,

150
00:07:36,957 --> 00:07:40,576
where a devil character follows the saint around.

151
00:07:40,576 --> 00:07:44,648
Krampus may be an ancient spirit that got baptised.

152
00:07:44,648 --> 00:07:47,815
Or a stage devil who got very,

153
00:07:47,815 --> 00:07:48,720
very popular.

154
00:07:49,120 --> 00:07:51,833
Either way, the partnership stuck.

155
00:07:51,833 --> 00:07:55,631
A kind saint and a terrifying servant.

156
00:07:55,631 --> 00:07:59,430
Reward and punishment, walking side by side,

157
00:07:59,430 --> 00:08:01,600
down every snowy street.

158
00:08:02,000 --> 00:08:04,257
And here's the twist.

159
00:08:04,257 --> 00:08:07,643
For centuries, nothing could stop Krampus.

160
00:08:07,643 --> 00:08:11,594
Not priests, not parents, not the Enlightenment.

161
00:08:11,594 --> 00:08:14,980
And then came the nineteen thirties.

162
00:08:15,380 --> 00:08:18,832
After nineteen thirty-two, Austria's authoritarian government,

163
00:08:18,832 --> 00:08:23,435
under Engelbert Dollfuss, turned against the Krampus tradition.

164
00:08:23,435 --> 00:08:29,763
In nineteen thirty-four, the New York Times even reported that Krampus

165
00:08:29,763 --> 00:08:32,640
was disliked in Fascist Austria.

166
00:08:33,040 --> 00:08:34,357
Did it work?

167
00:08:34,357 --> 00:08:37,429
Not really. These are remote mountain valleys.

168
00:08:37,429 --> 00:08:41,819
You cannot police every village on the fifth of December.

169
00:08:41,819 --> 00:08:46,208
Krampus simply went back to doing what he does best.

170
00:08:46,208 --> 00:08:49,720
Walking from house to house in the dark.

171
00:08:50,120 --> 00:08:55,313
Long before that, he'd already conquered the post office.

172
00:08:55,313 --> 00:08:59,929
From the eighteen hundreds, Austrians sent Krampus cards.

173
00:08:59,929 --> 00:09:01,660
Greetings from Krampus.

174
00:09:01,660 --> 00:09:05,699
Horned demons, chasing children, and the message,

175
00:09:05,699 --> 00:09:07,430
basically, happy holidays.

176
00:09:07,830 --> 00:09:10,760
And today he's more popular than ever.

177
00:09:10,760 --> 00:09:14,527
Krampus runs have spread across Europe and to America,

178
00:09:14,527 --> 00:09:19,131
with Hollywood films, beer labels, and costume clubs that spend all

179
00:09:19,131 --> 00:09:22,480
year building the scariest suit in the valley.

180
00:09:22,880 --> 00:09:25,768
So, how do you survive Krampus?

181
00:09:25,768 --> 00:09:29,618
Honestly, the folklore's answer is a little annoying.

182
00:09:29,618 --> 00:09:31,543
You don't fight him.

183
00:09:31,543 --> 00:09:33,467
You don't trap him.

184
00:09:33,467 --> 00:09:34,430
You behave.

185
00:09:34,830 --> 00:09:37,703
Krampus was never really a monster you defeat.

186
00:09:37,703 --> 00:09:38,780
He's a reminder.

187
00:09:38,780 --> 00:09:42,730
A way for long, dark winters to teach children that what

188
00:09:42,730 --> 00:09:46,680
you do all year comes due at the end of it.

189
00:09:47,080 --> 00:09:48,796
So, know the night.

190
00:09:48,796 --> 00:09:50,513
Stay near the saint.

191
00:09:50,513 --> 00:09:52,229
Have your answer ready.

192
00:09:52,229 --> 00:09:53,945
Respect the golden rod.

193
00:09:53,945 --> 00:09:57,807
And when you hear bells on a December street,

194
00:09:57,807 --> 00:10:00,810
take one step back from the kerb.

195
00:10:01,210 --> 00:10:05,365
That's five wards, from one of the oldest winter legends in

196
00:10:05,365 --> 00:10:08,387
Europe. Next week, we go somewhere much colder,

197
00:10:08,387 --> 00:10:11,786
to a monster that isn't sent by a saint,

198
00:10:11,786 --> 00:10:14,430
and doesn't care if you've been good.

199
00:10:14,830 --> 00:10:17,991
It's the creature of hunger.

200
00:10:17,991 --> 00:10:23,049
The Wendigo. And its rules are very different.

201
00:10:23,049 --> 00:10:29,372
Subscribe, so the Ward Book is open when it comes.

202
00:10:29,372 --> 00:10:34,430
And until then, stay inside on the fifth.
